  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 224 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nMoving into the upcoming weekend, the mid level ridge that has been \ncentered over the Carolinas and Tennessee River Valley will \ngradually flatten and retrograde westward as a potent mid level \ntrough dives southeastward through the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic \nbefore shifting into the Southeast and western Atlantic. While the \nmid level trough axis will generally remain to the north of the \nregion, this will help to keep South Florida situated in an area of \nweakness between two mid level ridges, one to the northwest over \nthe Plains, and one to the southeast well into the Atlantic. At \nthe surface, high pressure will gradually flatten and shift \nsouthward as a weakening frontal boundary stalls over the Gulf \nCoast states as well as Northern Florida through the weekend. \nWhile the synoptic wind flow will generally shift and become light\nout of the south southwest ahead of the frontal boundary during \nthe second half of the weekend, sea breezes will be the dominate \nfactor in wind direction heading into each afternoon. Plenty of \nmoisture will remain in place which will allow for scattered to \nnumerous showers and thunderstorms to develop each day during the \nupcoming weekend. While strong thunderstorm chances will remain \nrather limited, an isolated strong storm or two will remain \npossible especially where sea breeze and other mesoscale \nboundaries collide each afternoon. High temperatures over the \nweekend will generally rise into the lower 90s each day across \nmost of South Florida. Peak heat index values will generally range\nbetween 103 and 108 each afternoon. \n\nThe latest global and ensemble guidance suite shows this pattern \nsticking around heading into the early portion of next week as the \nmid level trough to the north shifts further into the Atlantic. The \ntrough axis will remain close enough to keep South Florida in an\narea of weakness between two mid level ridges. This will help to \nkeep the steering flow rather light and surface winds will \ngenerally remain sea breeze driven during this time frame. \nScattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms will develop each \nday with the highest chances occurring in the afternoon when sea \nbreeze and other mesoscale boundary collisions take place. High \ntemperatures will generally rise into the lower 90s each afternoon\nearly next week.",
